'\" t
.\" template.1 --- a starting page in roffsub; rename to your program.
.\" Replace "mytool" throughout, keep one sentence per line, write option
.\" hyphens as \-, and check with: roffsub -c mytool.1
.TH MYTOOL 1 @DATE@ "mytool @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mytool \- one line saying what mytool does
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBmytool\fR [\fB\-v\fR] [\fB\-o\fR \fIfile\fR] [\fIinput\fR ...]
\fBmytool\fR \fB\-h\fR | \fB\-\-help\fR
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mytool
reads each \fIinput\fR, or the standard input when none is named, and writes the result to the standard output.
.PP
A second paragraph explains the model the reader needs: what goes in, what comes out, and what is never changed.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-v
Report each \fIinput\fR on the standard error as it is read.
.TP
\fB\-o\fR \fIfile\fR
Write to \fIfile\fR instead of the standard output.
.TP
.B \-h
Print a short usage summary.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Show this manual.
.SH EXAMPLES
Process two files into one:
.PP
.nf
mytool \-o all.txt one.txt two.txt
.fi
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MYTOOL_OPTS
Options read before those on the command line.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TS
tab(@);
rb lb
r  l.
CODE@MEANING
_
0@success
1@T{
an input could not be read; the others were still processed
T}
2@a usage error
.TE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR roffsub (7)
